---
title: "Worked example: wages in the NLSW panel"
description: >
  A wage regression on 18,964 observations, from the calibrated level to
  clustered standard errors, a joint test, power and a report, in R and Stata.
---

```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("alphaN.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

The National Longitudinal Survey of Young Women followed a cohort of women
from 1968 on. Stata Press distributes an extract, so the code below runs as it
stands in either language. We regress log wages on age, experience, tenure,
where the respondent lives, race, education, marital status, union membership
and hours worked. With nearly 19,000 observations, *p*-values come cheap, and
this is exactly where a fixed level of .05 stops being informative.

## The data and the model

`r tabs_r("data")`

```{r data, message = FALSE}
library(alphaN)
library(haven)

nls <- read_dta("https://www.stata-press.com/data/r19/nlswork.dta")
nls$race <- factor(nls$race, labels = c("white", "black", "other"))

f <- ln_wage ~ age + ttl_exp + tenure + not_smsa + south + c_city + race +
  collgrad + msp + nev_mar + union + hours
```

`r tabs_stata("data")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "data")
```

`r tabs_end()`

## A *p*-value of .003 that favors the null

We start with the conventional standard errors. `JAB()` and `alphan jab`
report the Bayes factor of every coefficient.

`r tabs_r("ols")`

```{r ols}
m <- lm(f, data = nls)
n <- nobs(m)
n
round(summary(m)$coefficients[-1, ], 4)
signif(JAB(m), 3)
```

`r tabs_stata("ols")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "ols")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r ols-numbers, include = FALSE}
ct <- summary(m)$coefficients
bf <- JAB(m)
```

Look at `msp`, whether the respondent is married with the spouse present. Its
*p*-value is `r num(ct["msp", 4], 3, FALSE)`, which would earn two stars in most
tables. The Bayes factor is `r num(bf[["msp"]])`: the data are somewhat more
likely under the null hypothesis than under the alternative. This is Lindley's
paradox in an ordinary regression. (Bayes factors too large to store are `Inf` in R and about 8e+307 in Stata.)

The level that would have prevented this follows from the sample size alone,
so it can be set before the regression is run.

`r tabs_r("level")`

```{r level}
alphaN(n, BF = c(1, 3, 10))
```

`r tabs_stata("level")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "level")
```

`r tabs_end()`

For moderate evidence the coefficient has to clear a level of
`r num(alphaN(n, BF = 3), 5, FALSE)`, about
`r round(0.05/alphaN(n, BF = 3))` times stricter than .05.

## Clustered standard errors

Each woman appears in several survey years, so observations are not
independent. With cluster-robust standard errors the picture changes: `msp` is
no longer significant even at .05, and its Bayes factor drops to
`r local({ V <- sandwich::vcovCL(m, cluster = ~ idcode); num(JABt(n, ct["msp", 1]/sqrt(V["msp", "msp"])), 3) })`.

`r tabs_r("cluster")`

```{r cluster, message = FALSE}
library(sandwich)
library(lmtest)

V <- vcovCL(m, cluster = ~ idcode)
tc <- coeftest(m, vcov. = V)[-1, "t value"]
signif(JABt(n, tc), 3)
```

`r tabs_stata("cluster")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "cluster")
```

`r tabs_end()`

Wulff and Taylor (2024) recommend calibrating with the total number of
observations, the conservative choice, and then checking whether a conclusion
survives at the effective sample size, which deflates the total by the squared
ratio of the classical to the cluster-robust standard error. Take `nev_mar`,
never married.

`r tabs_r("neff")`

```{r neff}
se  <- sqrt(diag(vcov(m)))[["nev_mar"]]
rob <- sqrt(diag(V))[["nev_mar"]]
ne  <- n_effective(n, se, rob)
ne

alphaN(ne, BF = 3)
JABt(ne, tc[["nev_mar"]])
```

`r tabs_stata("neff")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "neff")
```

`r tabs_end()`

The effective sample size is less than a third of the total. The calibrated
level roughly doubles, and the Bayes factor rises to
`r num(JABt(ne, tc[["nev_mar"]]), 1)`. The coefficient clears the bar under
either sample size, so the conclusion does not hinge on the choice.

## A joint test

Is race related to wages at all? The test has two restrictions, so `q` is 2,
and `p` is the 12 parameters that remain in the model, the intercept included.
For joint tests the targeted effect is on Cohen's *f* scale, and
`sqrt(0.02)` is what Cohen (1988) calls a small effect.

`r tabs_r("joint")`

```{r joint}
m0 <- update(m, . ~ . - race)
Fs <- anova(m0, m)$F[2]
Fs

klauerBF(n, Fstat = Fs, q = 2, p = 12, de = sqrt(0.02))
alphaN(n, BF = 3, method = "ES", q = 2, p = 12, de = sqrt(0.02))
```

`r tabs_stata("joint")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "joint")
```

`r tabs_end()`

## How much does the method matter?

Here are all six calibrations at this sample size, for a target of 3. For the
two Klauer calibrations we target a standardized effect of 0.2 and give `p`,
the 13 other coefficients including the intercept.

`r tabs_r("methods")`

```{r methods}
c(sapply(c("JAB", "min", "robust", "balanced"),
         function(mm) alphaN(n, BF = 3, method = mm)),
  ES     = alphaN(n, BF = 3, method = "ES", de = 0.2, p = 13),
  moment = alphaN(n, BF = 3, method = "moment", de = 0.2, p = 13))
```

`r tabs_stata("methods")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "methods")
```

`r tabs_end()`

The levels span two orders of magnitude, from about .0001 under the moment
prior to about .01 under the balanced prior, so the method is a substantive
choice to be made and reported in advance. [Choosing a
calibration](choosing.html) goes through the options.

## Other models

Nothing here is specific to linear regression. `JAB()` accepts `glm()` fits and
`alphan jab` runs after any Stata estimator that stores `e(b)`, `e(V)` and
`e(N)`.

`r tabs_r("logit")`

```{r logit}
g <- glm(union ~ age + ttl_exp + tenure + not_smsa + south + c_city + race +
           collgrad + msp + nev_mar, data = nls, family = binomial)
signif(JAB(g), 3)
```

`r tabs_stata("logit")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "logit")
```

`r tabs_end()`

In this model `nev_mar` has a *p*-value of
`r num(summary(g)$coefficients["nev_mar", 4], 3, FALSE)` and a Bayes factor of
`r num(JAB(g)[["nev_mar"]])`. It would pass a conventional test while the data
do not distinguish the two hypotheses.

## What the level costs

A lower level means less power, and at design time the two should be chosen
together. The standardized effect `d` is what the *t* statistic divided by the
square root of the sample size estimates.

`r tabs_r("power")`

```{r power}
alphaN_power(n, d = c(0.02, 0.05, 0.1), BF = 3)
alphaN_power(n, d = c(0.02, 0.05, 0.1), BF = 3, method = "balanced")
```

`r tabs_stata("power")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "power")
```

`r tabs_end()`

With this sample the JAB calibration detects effects of 0.05 almost with
certainty and an effect of 0.02 only about a quarter of the time.

## Reporting

The report records the settings and the decision rule, ready to attach to a
preregistration.

`r tabs_r("report")`

```{r report}
alphaN_report(n, BF = 3, power_at = c(0.02, 0.05))
```

`r tabs_stata("report")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "report")
```

`r tabs_end()`

The mapping from *p*-values to Bayes factors at this sample size shows why a
.05 threshold would mislead: the whole range of *p*-values just below .05
corresponds to Bayes factors well below 1.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("wages", "figure", output = FALSE)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "", fig.alt = "The Bayes factor against the p-value at a sample size of 18,964, with the levels for Bayes factors of 1, 3 and 10 marked."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("wages-jabplot.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

## References

Cohen, J. (1988). *Statistical power analysis for the behavioral sciences*
(2nd ed.). Lawrence Erlbaum.

Klauer, K. C., Meyer-Grant, C. G., & Kellen, D. (2025). On Bayes factors for
hypothesis tests. *Psychonomic Bulletin & Review*, 32, 1070--1094.
<https://doi.org/10.3758/s13423-024-02612-2>

Wulff, J. N., & Taylor, L. (2024). How and why alpha should depend on sample
size: A Bayesian-frequentist compromise for significance testing. *Strategic
Organization*, 22(3), 550--581.
<https://doi.org/10.1177/14761270231214429>
